Sepulcher Dock
Ingredients: KRAUTROCK + K-INDIE + DARK, SINISTER & GOTHIC + Lead Vocal
Creative Catalyst: Its instruments are built from meteorite fragments and antique clockwork.

Sepulcher Dock sounds like an abandoned lighthouse broadcasting over shortwave radio from a foggy coast in late-night Seoul. It welds the relentless, hypnotic motorik drive of 1970s German experimentalism to the melancholic, bedroom-intimate chord progressions of modern underground Korean indie. Cloaked in a bruised, sepulchral gothic atmosphere, the music is anchored by a commanding lead vocal that moves seamlessly between haunting, cavernous operatic dirges and deadpan, spoken-word confessionals. The rhythmic engine chugs forward like a rusted freight train, while cavernous reverb and Victorian mourning lace wrap the production in an air of quiet dread.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 108 to 118 BPM; a heavy, relentless motorik beat driven by cyclical clockwork percussion and hollow wooden thuds.
- Harmonic Palette: Minor keys and pentatonic modes laced with dissonant microtones, heavy on sustained cello drones, minor-seventh guitar voicings, and icy synth pads.
- Production Signatures: 1) Real-time acoustic resonance captured from hollow metal meteorite chassis, 2) heavily compressed, whisper-close lead vocals competing with vast subterranean reverb, 3) tape-flutter saturation sourced from aging multi-track reel-to-reel machines.
- Dynamics: A slow-burn architecture where tracks start with a lone, ticking clockwork pulse and gradually layer hypnotic guitar arpeggios before exploding into a cathartic, thunderous wall of gothic noise.

Scene & Origin Lore
Born in the humid, subterranean basement clubs of Mapo-gu, Seoul, where vinyl-crate-digger students and rogue instrument builders congregated under flickering tungsten lights. Rejecting both slick corporate K-pop and conventional western indie tropes, this subculture adopted a uniform of ink-stained trench coats, velvet mourning ribbons, and heavy vintage wool. Rituals involve midnight listening sessions in former industrial dockyards where modified chronometers and celestial debris are played live through hacked guitar amplifiers, creating a sonic fog that feels both ancient and deeply futuristic.

Typical Instruments
- Modified electric guitars with meteorite-alloy pickups
- Clockwork-driven rhythm boxes and antique music-box movements
- Upright bass played with horsehair bows
- Monophonic analog synthesizers with tape-delay feedback loops
- Lead vocals (ranging from melancholic sighs to gothic baritone snarls)

